rtl8xxxu: TX A-MPDU off by default (tx_ampdu module parameter)

The VRX Pro's onboard RTL8188FTV stalled everything it sent for about
1 s, every few seconds: ping p99 942 ms / max 1001 ms on an idle link,
and scp onto the SD card stalling for seconds before finishing in a burst
(100 MB took 95 s, the sender's TCP in RTO backoff every 1-2 s).

Cause: rtl8xxxu starts a TX BlockAck session on every QoS TID, but it
has no per-frame TX status: rtl8xxxu_tx_complete() reports each frame as
acked once its USB transfer is done. When the chip gives up on a frame
(0.1-0.5 % here, at -36 dBm), mac80211 never learns it and never sends a
BlockAckReq, so the AP's reorder buffer holds every later frame of that
TID until its own timeout -- about 1 s on the bench TP-Link AP -- and
then releases them all at once. Measured on the unit (2026-11-15): all
13 one-way stalls of a 60 s UDP run started right after a lost datagram;
usbmon showed every frame handed to the chip on time (URB done in
< 0.4 ms), so nothing waited on the VRX Pro itself.

Without a BlockAck session a lost frame is just a lost frame (TCP
repairs it with SACK within an RTT). Same module, same AP:

                             tx_ampdu=1      tx_ampdu=0
  uplink one-way max         990-1032 ms     57 ms
  stalls > 200 ms / min      12              0
  100 MB onto the SD card    50-95 s         50 s
  100 MB off the SD card     46 s            65 s

Bulk sends from the VRX Pro (DVR downloads) lose about a third without
aggregation; rtl8xxxu.tx_ampdu=1 brings it back. The proper fix would
report per-frame TX status (Realtek CCX/TX report) so mac80211 sends the
BlockAckReq itself.

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtl8xxxu/rtl8xxxu_core.c	2026-11-15 20:40:48.039017556 +0100
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtl8xxxu/rtl8xxxu_core.c	2026-11-15 20:40:48.058478056 +0100
@@ -39,6 +39,13 @@
 static bool rtl8xxxu_dma_aggregation;
 static int rtl8xxxu_dma_agg_timeout = -1;
 static int rtl8xxxu_dma_agg_pages = -1;
+/*
+ * TX A-MPDU is off by default: this driver reports every frame as acked
+ * (it has no per-frame TX status), so mac80211 never sends a BlockAckReq
+ * for a frame the chip gave up on, and the AP's reorder buffer holds every
+ * later frame of that TID until its own timeout (about 1 s on a TP-Link AP).
+ */
+static bool rtl8xxxu_tx_ampdu;
 
 MODULE_AUTHOR("Jes Sorensen <Jes.Sorensen@gmail.com>");
 MODULE_DESCRIPTION("RTL8XXXu USB mac80211 Wireless LAN Driver");
@@ -64,6 +71,8 @@
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(dma_agg_timeout, "Set DMA aggregation timeout (range 1-127)");
 module_param_named(dma_agg_pages, rtl8xxxu_dma_agg_pages, int, 0600);
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(dma_agg_pages, "Set DMA aggregation pages (range 1-127, 0 to disable)");
+module_param_named(tx_ampdu, rtl8xxxu_tx_ampdu, bool, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(tx_ampdu, "Start TX A-MPDU (BlockAck) sessions (default off: no BAR on lost frames)");
 
 #define USB_VENDOR_ID_REALTEK		0x0bda
 #define RTL8XXXU_RX_URBS		32
@@ -5167,7 +5176,8 @@
 
 			ampdu_enable = true;
 
-			if (!test_bit(tid, priv->tx_aggr_started) &&
+			if (rtl8xxxu_tx_ampdu &&
+			    !test_bit(tid, priv->tx_aggr_started) &&
 			    !(skb->protocol == cpu_to_be16(ETH_P_PAE)))
 				if (!ieee80211_start_tx_ba_session(sta, tid, 0))
 					set_bit(tid, priv->tx_aggr_started);
@@ -6213,6 +6223,8 @@
 	switch (action) {
 	case IEEE80211_AMPDU_TX_START:
 		dev_dbg(dev, "%s: IEEE80211_AMPDU_TX_START\n", __func__);
+		if (!rtl8xxxu_tx_ampdu)
+			return -EOPNOTSUPP;
 		ampdu_factor = sta->deflink.ht_cap.ampdu_factor;
 		ampdu_density = sta->deflink.ht_cap.ampdu_density;
 		rtl8xxxu_set_ampdu_factor(priv, ampdu_factor);
